## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical tenant-scoped access control pattern using existing CNF primitives (sidecars, Redis) without overengineering.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant identity via header
- Redis-backed authorization cache
- Safe proxying in a sidecar

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: sidecar filter
const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
const path = req.path;
redis.hget(`${tenant}:allowed`, path, (err, ok) => {
  if (!ok) res.status(403).send('forbidden');
  else forward(req);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you seed and rotate the allowlist safely?
- How to handle missing tenant or Redis latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests resilience‑oriented CNF design for multi‑tenant traffic, focusing on per‑tenant state, timing windows, and safe recovery

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant state tracking within a sidecar
- Rolling window counters and cooldown handling
- 503 responses during open state, then auto‑recover

## Code Example
```javascript
class Breaker {
  constructor() { this.map = new Map(); }
  allow(tenant) {
    const s = this.map.get(tenant) || {f:0, t:0, open:0};
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now < s.open) return false;
    if (now - s.t > 60000) { s.f = 0; s.t = now; }
    const ok = s.f < 5;
    if (!ok) s.open = now + 30000;
    this.map.set(tenant, s);
    return ok;
  }
  fail(tenant) {
    const s = this.map.get(tenant) || {f:0, t:Date.now(), open:0};
    s.f += 1; s.t = Date.now(); this.map.set(tenant, s);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to test the breaker under load? Probe metrics and simulate bursts
- How to persist breaker state across restarts? Serializable store
- How to tune thresholds for different tenants?
